WEBVTT

00:00.629 --> 00:04.317
- [Voiceover] So, right
over here, we have the graph

00:04.317 --> 00:08.828
of the function y is equal
to x to the 3/2 power.

00:08.828 --> 00:13.828
And what I wanna do is find
the arc length of this curve,

00:14.811 --> 00:18.520
from when x equals zero
to when x is equal to--

00:18.520 --> 00:20.651
and I'm gonna pick a strange number here,

00:20.651 --> 00:21.693
and I picked this strange number

00:21.693 --> 00:23.923
'cause it makes the numbers
work out very well--

00:23.923 --> 00:26.740
to x is equal to 32/9.

00:26.740 --> 00:28.428
32/9 is, let's see...

00:28.428 --> 00:33.428
That's three and 5/9, so
it's gonna be right around...

00:35.815 --> 00:37.976
So that's three and 1/2, so
it's gonna be a little bit

00:37.976 --> 00:41.801
past three and 1/2, so it's
gonna be right over there.

00:41.801 --> 00:46.801
So we wanna find this arc
length right over here,

00:47.222 --> 00:49.764
this thing that I have depicted in yellow.

00:49.764 --> 00:54.155
So it's from zero to 32/9.

00:54.155 --> 00:55.949
And I encourage you to pause the video

00:55.949 --> 00:58.788
and try this out on your own.

00:58.788 --> 01:00.055
So I'm assuming you've had a go at it.

01:00.055 --> 01:01.702
And if at any point while
I'm working through it,

01:01.702 --> 01:05.128
you feel inspired, always
feel free to pause the video

01:05.128 --> 01:07.519
and continue working with it.

01:07.519 --> 01:09.734
So let's just apply the arc length formula

01:09.734 --> 01:13.155
that we got kind of a conceptual proof for

01:13.155 --> 01:15.592
in the previous video.

01:15.592 --> 01:17.691
So we know that the arc length...

01:17.691 --> 01:18.375
Let me write this.

01:18.375 --> 01:23.375
The arc length is going to be
equal to the definite integral

01:25.609 --> 01:30.609
from zero to 32/9 of the square root...

01:32.185 --> 01:35.475
Actually, let me just write
it in general terms first,

01:35.475 --> 01:38.694
so that you can kinda see the
formula and then how we apply it.

01:38.694 --> 01:43.694
So, it's the square root of one
plus f-prime of x squared d x.

01:46.469 --> 01:50.920
And in this case, it's going
to be the definite integral

01:50.920 --> 01:55.920
from zero to 32/9 of the
square root of one plus...

02:00.511 --> 02:01.557
Now, what's the derivative?

02:01.557 --> 02:06.557
If f of x is x to the 3/2,
then f-prime of x is going

02:09.052 --> 02:13.135
to be 3/2 x to the 1/2.

02:13.135 --> 02:15.128
And we picked this particular function

02:15.128 --> 02:18.426
because it simplifies
quite well when we put it

02:18.426 --> 02:20.690
under the radical, and
it's fairly straightforward

02:20.690 --> 02:22.513
to find the anti-derivative.

02:22.513 --> 02:24.946
So we've done a lot of
engineering of this problem

02:24.946 --> 02:26.394
to make the numbers work out well,

02:26.394 --> 02:27.797
but let's just go through it.

02:27.797 --> 02:32.517
So this is f-prime of x;
f-prime of x squared is going

02:32.517 --> 02:33.984
to be this quantity squared.

02:33.984 --> 02:38.600
It's going to be 9/4 x
to the 1/2 squared is x.

02:38.600 --> 02:43.600
So, one plus 9/4 x d x.

02:43.716 --> 02:45.850
And so, now, we just
have a definite integral

02:45.850 --> 02:48.323
that we know how to
solve this type of thing.

02:48.323 --> 02:50.589
And you might be able to
even do this in your head,

02:50.589 --> 02:52.241
essentially, do the u-substitution:

02:52.241 --> 02:54.583
say I have one plus 9/4 x.

02:54.583 --> 02:56.297
Its derivative is 9/4.

02:56.297 --> 02:58.895
I can kind of engineer that if I want,

02:58.895 --> 03:02.393
but instead, I'm just going to
do straight up u-substitution.

03:02.393 --> 03:07.393
So, if I say u is equal to one plus 9/4 x,

03:10.729 --> 03:11.610
then we know...

03:11.610 --> 03:12.830
Let's see.

03:12.830 --> 03:17.830
D u d x is going to be equal to 9/4.

03:19.579 --> 03:24.579
Or, we could say d u is equal to 9/4 d x.

03:24.808 --> 03:28.094
Or, we could say d x...

03:28.094 --> 03:30.180
Let me scroll down a little bit.

03:30.180 --> 03:32.438
We could say d x is equal to...

03:32.438 --> 03:36.149
I'm just gonna multiply
both sides times 4/9.

03:36.149 --> 03:38.854
Let's go to 4/9 d u.

03:38.854 --> 03:41.487
And then we just have to change
the bounds of integration.

03:41.487 --> 03:46.487
When x is equal to zero, then
u is going to be equal to...

03:48.781 --> 03:51.262
9/4 times zero is just zero, so u is going

03:51.262 --> 03:52.936
to be equal to one.

03:52.936 --> 03:57.936
And when x is equal to 32/9--

03:59.975 --> 04:01.458
and this is why that number was picked--

04:01.458 --> 04:03.602
what's u going to be equal to?

04:03.602 --> 04:07.936
32/9 times 9/4 is gonna be 32/4,

04:07.936 --> 04:09.992
which is going to be eight plus one.

04:09.992 --> 04:13.011
So that worked out very
nicely; imagine that.

04:13.011 --> 04:13.797
So there we have it.

04:13.797 --> 04:17.636
So, this is going to be equal
to the definite integral...

04:17.636 --> 04:18.998
Actually, let me make
it clear that this is

04:18.998 --> 04:20.349
what is equal to this.

04:20.349 --> 04:25.349
The definite integral
from u is equal to one,

04:25.517 --> 04:27.589
to u is equal to 9--

04:27.589 --> 04:28.922
I'm gonna make it very
explicit that I'm dealing

04:28.922 --> 04:30.839
with u now--

04:30.839 --> 04:35.488
of the square root of u.

04:35.488 --> 04:40.152
And instead of d x, we
have d x is 4/9 d u.

04:40.152 --> 04:43.760
Let me do it this way: the square root...

04:43.760 --> 04:45.909
Whoops, that's not the right color.

04:45.909 --> 04:50.909
Square root of u; instead of
d x, we have times 4/9 d u.

04:52.880 --> 04:57.880
And I'm just gonna take the
4/9 and stick it out here.

04:58.178 --> 05:01.597
And we know how to apply the fundamental,

05:01.597 --> 05:04.329
or, I guess, the second fundamental
theorem of calculus here,

05:04.329 --> 05:06.663
to evaluate this definite integral.

05:06.663 --> 05:11.663
This is going to be 4/9
times the anti-derivative

05:12.446 --> 05:15.030
of the square root of u,
which is the same thing

05:15.030 --> 05:16.551
as u to the 1/2.

05:16.551 --> 05:19.564
It's going to be u to the
3/2, and then we divide

05:19.564 --> 05:23.204
by 3/2, which is the same
thing as multiplying by 2/3.

05:23.204 --> 05:26.371
We're going to evaluate
that at u equals nine

05:26.371 --> 05:28.886
and u is equal to one.

05:28.886 --> 05:31.427
And so, we're in the home stretch here.

05:31.427 --> 05:36.427
This is going to be equal
to 4/9 times 2/3 times 9

05:41.641 --> 05:46.641
to the 3/2 minus 2/3 times one to the 3/2.

05:49.636 --> 05:52.119
So, 9 to the 3/2, that is...

05:52.119 --> 05:54.724
Let's see: the square
root of nine is three,

05:54.724 --> 05:57.302
to the third power is 27.

05:57.302 --> 05:59.352
And this, of course, is one.

05:59.352 --> 06:03.436
So we are left with 2/3 of...

06:03.436 --> 06:04.920
Actually, let's just factor out the 2/3,

06:04.920 --> 06:07.117
that makes it easier.

06:07.117 --> 06:12.117
This is going to be equal to
2/3 times 4/9 is equal to 8/27.

06:15.355 --> 06:17.176
I've just factored out the 2/3.

06:17.176 --> 06:19.777
And then we're gonna have 27 minus one

06:19.777 --> 06:23.274
inside, I guess you could
say the brackets now.

06:23.274 --> 06:28.274
So 27 minus one is just going to be 26.

06:29.803 --> 06:32.157
Times 26.

06:32.157 --> 06:36.212
And we could obviously
simplify this more if we want.

06:36.212 --> 06:39.173
Eight times 26 is...

06:39.173 --> 06:41.154
So, actually, let's just
figure that out, just for fun.

06:41.154 --> 06:42.792
So, eight times 26 is going

06:42.792 --> 06:47.792
to be 160 plus eight times six
is 48, so it's 208 over 27.

06:56.910 --> 07:01.910
And we are done.
